</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xintaowu@uark.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid evolution of social media has provided enhanced communication channels for individuals to create online content, enabling them to express their thoughts and opinions. Multimodal memes, often utilized for playful or humorous expressions with visual and textual elements, are sometimes misused to disseminate hate speech against individuals or groups. While the detection of hateful memes is well-researched, developing effective methods to transform hateful content in memes remains a significant challenge. Leveraging the powerful generation and reasoning capabilities of Vision-Language Models (VLMs), we address the tasks of detecting and mitigating hateful content. This paper presents two key contributions: first, a definition-guided prompting technique for detecting hateful memes, and second, a unified framework for mitigating hateful content in memes, named <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span>, which works by replacing hateful textual and/or visual components. With our definition-guided prompts, VLMs achieve impressive performance on hateful memes detection task. Furthermore, our <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> framework, integrated with VLMs, demonstrates a strong capability to convert hateful memes into non-hateful forms that meet human-level criteria for hate speech and maintain multimodal coherence between image and text. Through empirical experiments, we show the effectiveness of state-of-the-art pretrained VLMs such as LLaVA, Gemini and GPT-4o on the proposed tasks, providing a comprehensive analysis of their respective strengths and limitations for these tasks. This paper aims to shed light on important applications of VLMs for ensuring safe and respectful online environments.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Warning: This paper contains examples of hateful content.</span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Nowadays, social media platforms serve as extensive repositories for a wide range of content, including text, images, audio, and video. However, this vast array of information presents a dual challenge: it may not always be safe for viewers, potentially containing explicit or implicit harm to viewers. Multimodal memes, often utilized for playful or humorous expression with visual and textual elements, are sometimes misappropriated to disseminate hate speech against individuals or groups based on their characteristics. Consequently, identifying hateful memes has become a critical issue that social media platforms aim to address to protect users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Detecting hateful memes, however, is a non-trivial task since multimodal memes combine visual and textual components to convey messages. The task is more challenging when the hate can be either unimodal or multimodal. While unimodal hate can be identified by assessing the hatefulness in either the image or the text modality, multimodal hate is more difficult to detect because the image and text are non-hateful individually but together convey a hateful message. Further, the hateful content is not always explicitly expressed but is implicitly hidden within a historical story behind it. Therefore, intelligent systems are urgently needed to process the vast digital content uploaded to social platforms. Many deep learning techniques have been proposed for this detection task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. While significant research has been dedicated to detecting hateful memes, there is a notable gap in methodologies for converting unintentionally hateful memes into more appropriate versions. Addressing this gap represents the next important step in hate speech research, highlighting the potential to assist online users in creating safe and lighthearted memes to be shared on social media.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable performance across various natural language processing tasks in recent years. With a strong ability to learn human language from vast corpora of text, LLMs can encode enormous amounts of knowledge as embedding vectors, which is a backbone for transforming the knowledge to different forms of representations with the help of adapters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> or decoder modules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. This flexibility allows AI practitioners to combine text with other sources of information to build multimodally large models. When integrated with vision capabilities, LLMs enable the exploration of emergent abilities in multimodal contexts. Vision-language models (VLMs), such as CLIP, LLaVA, Flamingo, GPT-4, and Gemini, have demonstrated impressive performance across various vision-linguistic tasks. VLMs and their variations can not only perform well on general tasks but also be applied in other fields such as medical data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. While VLMs have gained traction in different fields, socially responsible problems remain under-explored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose VLM-based approaches for detecting and mitigating hateful memes, making two key contributions: (1) a definition-guided prompting technique for detecting hateful memes and (2) a unified framework for mitigating hateful content in memes. To the best of our knowledge, our work is among the first few works to leverage prompting techniques with VLMs for the detection of hateful memes and the first to explore the hateful memes mitigation – a critical step towards ensuring safe and respectful interactions on online social media environments in the era of LLMs and VLMs. For example, users may share memes intended to be humorous or satirical, but there remains a major concern whether such content, either explicitly or implicitly, conveys harmful messages to viewers. More importantly, if memes are determined to be hateful, an open and intriguing question arises: <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">what actions can be taken to assist social media users in combating toxic content and promoting positive messages?</span> As a result, we focus on exploring a novel strategy for modifying hateful memes such that the mitigated versions convey meaningful messages without being offensive, addressing a challenging societal issue that requires interdisciplinary collaboration between AI and social science researchers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To effectively detect hateful memes, we propose a definition-guided prompt that incorporates a detailed definition of hatefulness and clear classification criteria to enhance the reasoning capabilities of VLMs. With our robust detection prompts, VLMs can outperform multiple hateful memes detection baselines without the need for additional fine-tuning steps or OCR text. Notably, our results reveal that VLMs yield substantial improvements in AUROC scores with our proposed prompts, compared to prior findings with simpler prompts discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To tackle the challenge of mitigating hateful content, we propose a mitigation framework, named <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span>, for converting hateful memes to non-hateful ones by leveraging VLMs with task-specific prompts. Our framework decomposes the task of mitigating hateful memes into multiple sequential steps, including tasks such as analyzing the type of hate, identifying the source of hate, and generating substitute image or text. We employ GPT-4o as the central reasoning component in our mitigation framework due to its demonstrated superior performance and exceptional generative capabilities, as evidenced by our comprehensive experiments on hateful meme detection. Nonetheless, the framework is modular and can be easily adapted to integrate other state-of-the-art VLMs. To evaluate the hateful memes mitigation task, we first use our unified framework to transform hateful memes into non-hateful versions and create a corresponding dataset of mitigated memes. Human evaluation is then adopted to assess the quality of the mitigated dataset based on two metrics: <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-hatefulness</span> and <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">image-text coherence</span>. With exceptional generative capabilities, we incorporate VLMs like GPT-4o into our framework as the primary reasoning component for the aforementioned tasks. Our experimental results indicate that the <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> framework effectively transforms hateful memes into non-hateful versions by removing offensive visual and/or textual elements and suggesting new alternatives while ensuring multimodal image-text coherence in the new memes. This marks a substantial advancement over previous deep learning models, which lacked the capacity for such content generation.
To facilitate research reproducibility, an anonymous folder containing our source code and data is available at <a href="https://tinyurl.com/unhatememe" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">https://tinyurl.com/unhatememe</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Hate Speech Detection</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There has been a concerted effort from the AI research community to address hate speech problems, including hate speech detection datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> or hateful memes detection challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. To deal with the hate speech detection task, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> proposed a framework to detect harmful text by evaluating coded words, which silently represent hateful meanings on social media, by applying a two-layer network on contextual embeddings from ELMo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. BERT-MRP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> proposed a two-stage BERT-based model, including masked rationale prediction (MRP) and hate speech detection. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> also evaluated Open Pretrain Transformer (OPT) on the hate speech detection task via zero-, one-, or few-shot cases. Besides hateful text, hatefulness can exist in multimodal form. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> introduced a challenge about detecting hate speech in multimodal memes. With enormous baselines on three categories: unimodal models from computer vision or natural language processing tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, multimodal models by combining smaller unimodally pretrained models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, or multimodal models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the challenge still calls for more contribution from communities. Many state-of-the-art methods for detecting hateful memes have been proposed during the challenge. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> proposed an ensemble approach of one or multiple vision and language models to get the final prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent efforts have been directed toward hate speech moderation utilizing Large Language Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Prompting methods have been proposed to classify hateful memes with pretrained language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Ji et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> proposed incorporating visual captioning and attribute extraction to enhance the zero-shot detection performance of LLMs. In the few-shot learning setting, Cao et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> proposed a modularized network that effectively detects hateful memes by fine-tuning LLMs through parameter-efficient techniques. Furthermore, generative models have been employed to create memes with varying objectives. For example, MemeCraft <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> was designed to generate memes promoting climate action and gender equality, while UnsafeDiffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> focused on generating unsafe images and hateful memes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Vision-Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to traditional deep learning models specifically trained for classification tasks, Vision-Language Models have been catching much attention over many interesting applications. CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> proposed a new network to connect image and text data. The method guides the model in learning visual concepts in classification tasks using supervision from natural language. OpenAI GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, Google Gemini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, Flamingo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, OpenFlamingo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> demonstrated their effectiveness on various vision-language tasks such as question-answering or human-like chatbot. Along with groundbreaking large foundation models, many research works have explored the extension of large models to different domains.
LLaVA-Med <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and Med-PaLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> can analyze the medical images and answer related questions like a human expert.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Hateful Memes Detection</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Definition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> contains multiple pairs of data sample <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>’s, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input meme and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are label of hateful or non-hateful, respectively. For memes dataset, each input image <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the visual part <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the textual part <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the task of hateful memes detection as a classification problem <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OCR for meme text.</span> In general, a discrete visual element <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or textual element <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is not always available. With online memes, the meme text is often integrated into the image itself, resulting in the final meme <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Previous research on hateful meme detection has utilized Optical Character Recognition (OCR) methods to extract the meme text, thereby aiding models in better analyzing the hatefulness within the textual modality. While recent state-of-the-art VLMs have shown excellent capabilities in extracting texts from images without the need for additional OCR modules, we also evaluate the setting where OCR-extracted text is available. This approach allows for a comprehensive comparison with previous studies and an assessment of how VLMs perform both with and without the use of OCR.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Challenges with multimodal memes.</span> Although <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary classification task (hateful or non-hateful), accurately detecting hateful memes is non-trivial due to the presence of both unimodal and multimodal hate. Hateful memes with unimodal hate can be identified by assessing the hate in either image or text individually. However, hateful memes with multimodal hate pose a more complex challenge, as the meme image <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and text <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-hateful on their own but together convey a hateful message. This necessitates an effective approach for interpreting the message conveyed by both modalities. To address these challenges, we introduce definition-guided prompts to guide VLMs in detecting hateful memes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Definition-guided Prompt</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While one can prompt the VLM to enlarge its capabilities on various tasks, it is essential to note that the model’s performance dramatically depends on the quality of prompts, including clear instructions for the specific task. Detecting hateful memes presents a unique challenge in crafting an effective prompt. This challenge involves deciphering slang or historical references within visual and textual elements to identify a hateful message. Another challenging problem is how to guide the model to effectively reason about the source of hate in the memes. In Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2 Definition-guided Prompt ‣ 3 Hateful Memes Detection ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we illustrate an example where GPT-4o demonstrates a strong ability to describe the meme, yet it fails to detect hateful meme using a simple prompt as used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="147.79" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 147.79" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,147.79) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 141.89 C 0 145.15 2.64 147.79 5.91 147.79 L 471.47 147.79 C 474.73 147.79 477.38 145.15 477.38 141.89 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 141.89 C 1.97 144.06 3.73 145.82 5.91 145.82 L 471.47 145.82 C 473.65 145.82 475.41 144.06 475.41 141.89 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.67 61.12)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.25em;--ltx-fo-height:5.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:3.79em;font-size:10pt;" height="130.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 78)" width="460.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:33.25em;">
<span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:85.4pt;"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/35279.png" id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:118/78;" width="118" height="78" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></span><span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:243.1pt;">
<span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User:</span><span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Is this meme hateful? Answer:
</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Assistant:</span><span id="S3.F1.pic1.p1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> No, this meme isn’t hateful. It’s a humorous piece depicting a couple dancing, with a playful caption about converting a dishwasher into a weed eater. It seems intended to be lighthearted and silly.</span></span>
</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An example of GPT-4o failing to detect hateful meme.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A comprehensive definition of hate is, therefore, necessary. We adapt the definition of hate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> as part of the instructions in our prompt. Subsequently, we provide the VLM with guidelines on how to classify memes as hateful or non-hateful by illustrating the strategy for analyzing both the visual and textual components and how to combine them to achieve the desired outcome. In Figure <a href="#S3.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 3.3 Definition-guided Few-shot Prompt ‣ 3 Hateful Memes Detection ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>, we introduce our task-specific zero-shot prompt, referred to as definition-guided prompt, for detecting hateful memes using VLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Definition-guided Few-shot Prompt</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our definition-guided prompt can be adapted to a few-shot setting by incorporating multiple relevant in-context examples to improve detection. The performance of in-context learning heavily relies on the quality of these examples, as those closely related to each test item are more effective representatives for inference. For each test meme, we use Retrieval-based In-Context Example Selection (RICES) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> to find similar demonstration memes from a candidate set. Since cloud VLMs like Gemini or GPT-4o don’t provide visual embeddings, we use the CLIP model’s visual encoder to obtain meme embeddings. We then calculate and rank cosine similarities between test and candidate memes to select the best demonstration set, ensuring a balanced number of examples per class. Figure <a href="#S3.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 3.3 Definition-guided Few-shot Prompt ‣ 3 Hateful Memes Detection ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a> shows a prompt example with four demonstrations, with the full prompt detailed in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompts ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F2.sf1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="329.18" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 329.18" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,329.18) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#008080;" fill="#008080" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 323.27 C 0 326.53 2.64 329.18 5.91 329.18 L 471.47 329.18 C 474.73 329.18 477.38 326.53 477.38 323.27 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9FFD9;" fill="#D9FFD9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 323.27 C 1.97 325.45 3.73 327.21 5.91 327.21 L 471.47 327.21 C 473.65 327.21 475.41 325.45 475.41 323.27 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.67 12.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.25em;--ltx-fo-height:22.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="311.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 308.38)" width="460.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.F2.sf1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.25em;">
<span id="S3.F2.sf1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User:<span id="S3.F2.sf1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Hatefulness definition: Hate speech is defined as a direct or indirect attack on people based on characteristics, including ethnicity, race, nationality, immigration status, religion, caste, sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, disease, mocking hate crime or evoking pain from historical figures. The definition of attack includes violent or dehumanizing speech, statements of inferiority, and calls for exclusion or segregation.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">Classification criteria: A meme is considered hateful if either the image or text is hateful on its own. The meme is also hateful if both the image and text are not hateful individually, but together convey a hateful message. Conversely, if neither the image nor the text is hateful and they do not together create a hateful meaning, the meme is not considered hateful.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-test-meme&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>You consider visual and textual meanings to classify the meme as hateful or non-hateful based on the hatefulness definition and classification criteria.
After giving a short explanation, provide the answer with either hateful or non-hateful in the following format:
<br class="ltx_break">Classification:
<br class="ltx_break">Probability of the meme being hateful (from 0 to 1):</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Definition-guided zero-shot prompt.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="278.98" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 278.98" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,278.98) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#008080;" fill="#008080" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 273.07 C 0 276.34 2.64 278.98 5.91 278.98 L 471.47 278.98 C 474.73 278.98 477.38 276.34 477.38 273.07 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9FFD9;" fill="#D9FFD9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 273.07 C 1.97 275.25 3.73 277.01 5.91 277.01 L 471.47 277.01 C 473.65 277.01 475.41 275.25 475.41 273.07 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.67 11.75)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.25em;--ltx-fo-height:18.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.22em;font-size:10pt;" height="261.64" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 258.56)" width="460.09"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.25em;">
<span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User:<span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;similar-to-definition-guided-prompt&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break">Here are some demonstrations on classifying memes:

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-demonstration-meme-1&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Classification: <span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;label-1&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-demonstration-meme-2&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Classification: <span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;label-2&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-demonstration-meme-3&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Classification: <span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;label-3&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-demonstration-meme-4&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Classification: <span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;label-4&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;similar-to-definition-guided-prompt&gt;</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Definition-guided few-shot prompt.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Our prompts for hateful memes detection.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Hateful Memes Mitigation</h2>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00150v1/illustrative_figure_meme_mitigation.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:452/607;" width="452" height="607" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of <span id="S4.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> framework for mitigating hateful content in memes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Problem Definition</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{H}\subset\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{H}\subset\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be a subset including hateful memes in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},y_{i})\in\mathcal{D}_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{i})\in\mathcal{D}_{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}=(v_{i},t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}=(v_{i},t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> (hateful). We define the task of mitigating hateful content in memes as the problem of finding suitable substitutions, represented by the function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\prime}\times\mathcal{T}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\prime}\times\mathcal{T}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given that a meme typically consists of both visual and textual components, the function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is designed to address two distinct tasks: <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, responsible for finding a substitute text <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}^{\prime}=g_{t}(v_{i},t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}^{\prime}=g_{t}(v_{i},t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, responsible for finding a substitute image <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}^{\prime}=g_{v}(v_{i},t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}^{\prime}=g_{v}(v_{i},t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The objective is to convert the hateful meme <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into a modified version <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{\prime}=(v_{i}^{\prime},t_{i}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{\prime}=(v_{i}^{\prime},t_{i}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be classified as non-hateful, i.e, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The content generation capabilities of VLMs can be leveraged to address these tasks. Specifically, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approached as a text generation task by directly prompting GPT-4o to generate a substitute text. Designing the task <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is more complicated as it requires multiple steps to obtain the substitute image. In particular, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> involves prompting the VLM to create a description of the potential substitute image, followed by matching this description against a collection of non-hateful images to identify the most suitable substitution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mitigation criteria.</span> The objective of the mitigation task is to modify hateful memes to make them suitable for sharing on social networks. These memes aim to spread playful and lighthearted content without being offensive to other users. Additionally, the framework must ensure coherence between image and text in the newly created meme. For example, a naïve method might simply replace the original text in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2 Definition-guided Prompt ‣ 3 Hateful Memes Detection ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with “<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It’s a rainy day!</span>” – a substitution that fails to convey any meaningful message when paired with the image. Thus, we anticipate the proposed framework to satisfy two criteria for the mitigated memes: <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-hatefulness</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">image-text coherence</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Challenges with mitigating hateful content.</span> Developing an effective generation method <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> alone is insufficient for a practical application due to the challenge of determining whether the image or text modality requires correction. In other words, users must manually specify either <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> (for image) or <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (for text), or both, to perform the generation – a process that ideally should be automated by the mitigation framework. Therefore, we establish that an effective framework for mitigating hateful memes should be unified and capable of performing the task in an end-to-end manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem Definition ‣ 3 Hateful Memes Detection ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the hate in memes can be either unimodal or multimodal, depending on how the hateful content is embedded in the visual and textual components. The decision on whether to address the image or text modality hinges on the type of hate depicted in the memes. For a meme with unimodal hate, which often explicitly contains hateful image or text, the corresponding hateful components can be corrected to mitigate the hate. Additionally, there are memes in which both the image and text independently express hate. For a meme with multimodal hate, altering either modality removes the hateful message, as it only emerges from the specific combination of image and text. Thus, distinguishing between unimodal and multimodal hate is a crucial step in the framework. Once the type of hate in memes is identified, the next step for unimodal hate cases is to determine which modality (image or text) is responsible for the hateful content. These challenges lead to two additional tasks beyond the main generation task: analyzing the type of hate, denoted as <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\{\mathrm{unimodal-hate},\mathrm{multimodal-hate}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>unimodal</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>hate</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>multimodal</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>hate</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\{\mathrm{unimodal-hate},\mathrm{multimodal-hate}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and identifying the source of hate, denoted as <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\{\mathrm{image},\mathrm{text},\mathrm{both}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>image</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>text</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>both</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\{\mathrm{image},\mathrm{text},\mathrm{both}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Unified Framework for Mitigating Hateful Content in Memes</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective of mitigating hateful memes involves transforming hateful content into non-hateful versions while preserving the multimodal coherence between visual and textual elements. As aforementioned, a viable framework for mitigating hateful content in memes should automatically handle three tasks: analyzing the type of hate, identifying the source of hate, and generating substitute image or text. To this end, we propose a unified and end-to-end framework called <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span>. This framework processes hateful memes and generates non-hateful counterparts with minimal alterations, ensuring that the content remains as faithful to the original as possible. We leverage the advanced generative capabilities of VLMs, such as GPT-4o, and propose task-specific prompts to guide the model toward achieving the desired outcome. Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> offers an illustrative overview of our <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> framework.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.F4.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F4.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="425.35" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 229.14 425.35" width="229.14"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,425.35) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#008080;" fill="#008080" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 419.44 C 0 422.7 2.64 425.35 5.91 425.35 L 223.23 425.35 C 226.49 425.35 229.14 422.7 229.14 419.44 L 229.14 5.91 C 229.14 2.64 226.49 0 223.23 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9FFD9;" fill="#D9FFD9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 419.44 C 1.97 421.61 3.73 423.38 5.91 423.38 L 223.23 423.38 C 225.41 423.38 227.17 421.61 227.17 419.44 L 227.17 5.91 C 227.17 3.73 225.41 1.97 223.23 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.67 8.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:15.31em;--ltx-fo-height:29.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="408" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 408)" width="211.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:15.31em;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User:<span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Hatefulness definition: …
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;similar-to-definition-guided-prompt&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break">Classification Criteria: A meme is classified as unimodal-hate if either the image or the text is individually hateful. Conversely, a meme is classified as multimodal-hate if neither the image nor the text is hateful when considered individually, but together they convey a hateful message.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.pic1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-test-meme&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>The provided meme is considered hateful. To classify it as unimodal-hate or multimodal-hate, you analyze the hate in each of image and text parts individually based on the provided hatefulness definition. Then you give the answer based on the classification criteria in the following format.
<br class="ltx_break">Explanation:
<br class="ltx_break">Classification:</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<span class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt for analyzing type of hate.</span></span>
</span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="159.67" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 229.14 159.67" width="229.14"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,159.67) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#008080;" fill="#008080" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 153.77 C 0 157.03 2.64 159.67 5.91 159.67 L 223.23 159.67 C 226.49 159.67 229.14 157.03 229.14 153.77 L 229.14 5.91 C 229.14 2.64 226.49 0 223.23 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9FFD9;" fill="#D9FFD9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 153.77 C 1.97 155.94 3.73 157.71 5.91 157.71 L 223.23 157.71 C 225.41 157.71 227.17 155.94 227.17 153.77 L 227.17 5.91 C 227.17 3.73 225.41 1.97 223.23 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.67 8.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:15.31em;--ltx-fo-height:10.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="142.33" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 142.33)" width="211.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:15.31em;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User:<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-test-meme&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>The provided meme is considered hateful. Your task is to analyze whether the source of hate inside the meme is from image or text or both. Please answer with “hate from image”, “hate from text”, or “hate from both”.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<span class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt for identifying source of hate.</span></span>
</span></span></td>
<td id="S4.F4.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="378.22" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 229.14 378.22" width="229.14"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,378.22) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#008080;" fill="#008080" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 372.32 C 0 375.58 2.64 378.22 5.91 378.22 L 223.23 378.22 C 226.49 378.22 229.14 375.58 229.14 372.32 L 229.14 5.91 C 229.14 2.64 226.49 0 223.23 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9FFD9;" fill="#D9FFD9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 372.32 C 1.97 374.49 3.73 376.25 5.91 376.25 L 223.23 376.25 C 225.41 376.25 227.17 374.49 227.17 372.32 L 227.17 5.91 C 227.17 3.73 225.41 1.97 223.23 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.67 11.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:15.31em;--ltx-fo-height:25.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="360.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 358.19)" width="211.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:15.31em;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User:<span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-test-meme&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>The provided meme is considered hateful. Your task is to analyze its hatefulness and generate a new image such that the new meme is not hateful, using the same meme text. Please ensure the generated image aligns with the content of the text. Please only describe the new image that you want to generate in a short sentence only.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Assistant:<span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;generated-description&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break"></span><span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CLIP <math id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> find <math id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> matching images</span><span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">

<br class="ltx_break"></span>User:<span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-<math id="S4.F4.sf3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-matching-images&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>You are given a collection of substitute images. Please choose the best image such that the new meme delivers non-hateful, positive and humorous messages.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<span class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt for generating substitute image.</span></span>
</span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf4" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F4.sf4.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="228.78" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 229.14 228.78" width="229.14"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,228.78) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#008080;" fill="#008080" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 222.88 C 0 226.14 2.64 228.78 5.91 228.78 L 223.23 228.78 C 226.49 228.78 229.14 226.14 229.14 222.88 L 229.14 5.91 C 229.14 2.64 226.49 0 223.23 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9FFD9;" fill="#D9FFD9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 222.88 C 1.97 225.05 3.73 226.81 5.91 226.81 L 223.23 226.81 C 225.41 226.81 227.17 225.05 227.17 222.88 L 227.17 5.91 C 227.17 3.73 225.41 1.97 223.23 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.67 11.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:15.31em;--ltx-fo-height:15.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="211.44" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 208.75)" width="211.85"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F4.sf4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:15.31em;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.sf4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User:<span id="S4.F4.sf4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
<span id="S4.F4.sf4.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-test-meme&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>The provided meme is considered hateful. Your task is to analyze its hatefulness and generate substitute text such that the new meme is not hateful, using the same meme image. Please ensure the generated text aligns with the content of the image and that the tone of the text is similar. Please only provide the new text that you want to generate.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<span class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt for generating substitute text.</span></span>
</span></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompts for mitigating hateful content in memes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span>, GPT-4o serves as the central component for executing various tasks. When mitigating hateful content in memes, our initial step involves prompting GPT-4o to analyze the type of hate. The specific prompt used for this task is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Unified Framework for Mitigating Hateful Content in Memes ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>. Similar to the hateful memes detection task, we first construct the prompt for the task of analyzing the type of hate <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> using task-specific hatefulness definition and classification criteria. A detailed and well-structured prompt is crucial, as the outcome of this task will significantly impact the subsequent steps in the process. In Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we show three examples of hateful memes, where the first two memes <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> present unimodal hate, and the third meme <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> presents multimodal hate. If a meme is determined to express hate in a unimodal manner, the next task is to identify its source of hate with the task <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. The prompt for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Unified Framework for Mitigating Hateful Content in Memes ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>. There are three cases that can cause hate in unimodal-hate memes: hate from the image, hate from the text, and hate from both the image and text<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Due to space limit, we omit this case from Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and provide an example in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Hateful Memes Mitigation Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</span></span></span>. The first two scenarios require a single generative process, whereas the latter necessitates separate generative processes for both components. Memes classified as exhibiting multimodal hate do not require this step because generating a substitution for either the image or the text can remove the hateful message, depending on the user’s choice. From there, our framework proceeds to perform the generation task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The generation task is the most crucial component effectively transforming hatefulness in memes. For memes requiring image substitution <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, we propose a multi-step prompting approach. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the process begins with prompting GPT-4o to assess the level of hatefulness within a given meme and to describe the substitute image it intends to generate. Subsequently, we employ the CLIP model, trained for image-text alignment, to locate <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> images that closely match GPT-4o’s description. The embeddings of images in the substitute collection are precomputed using the CLIP visual encoder. The embeddings of the images in the substitute collection are precomputed using the CLIP visual encoder. Following this, we compute the similarities between the text embedding of the generated description and the precomputed image embeddings. The images that best match the description are then forwarded to GPT-4o along with a second prompt, tasked with selecting the most suitable image for substitution. The detailed multi-step prompt is depicted in Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf3" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Unified Framework for Mitigating Hateful Content in Memes ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(c)</span></a>. For generating text substitutions <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we utilize the prompt depicted in Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf4" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Unified Framework for Mitigating Hateful Content in Memes ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(d)</span></a>, sending it to GPT-4o to produce a new text. In both scenarios, we design the prompts to ensure that mitigated memes are devoid of hateful content and their image-text coherence is maintained. Ultimately, the framework integrates generated and/or original components, outputting the corresponding mitigated memes: <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with image substitution, and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> text substitution, as presented in the final module of Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Before creating the mitigated meme, the original text must be removed from the existing meme. While various object removal methods, such as Image Inpainting, typically require input masks for the objects to be removed, manually drawing these masks is not efficient. To address this issue, we propose using a module that integrates GroundingDINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and Inpainting Anything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. GroundingDINO is initially employed to extract the bounding boxes of objects described by an input text. In our scenario, we focus on extracting the bounding boxes of text lines present on the given meme. However, bounding boxes alone are insufficiently detailed for creating precise masks to remove text, as they might lead to the unexpected removal of other objects. Therefore, in the second step, we utilize the Segment Anything Model (SAM) to segment the specific textual areas within the extracted bounding boxes. Once we have generated these detailed segmented text masks, we utilize Inpaint Anything framework, specifically LaMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, to effectively remove the text lines from the memes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset.</span>
We conduct evaluations using the Hateful Memes Challenge (HMC) Dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. This dataset comprises 8,500 memes in the training set and 1,000 memes in the seen test set for Phase I, and includes 2,000 memes in the unseen test set for Phase II. Each meme in the dataset is categorized as either hateful or non-hateful.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> For the detection task, we evaluate our approach against 22 baseline models, including 11 competition baselines and five winners from the challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> that used deep learning techniques. We also compare our results with six benchmark studies on this dataset with Flamingo (Fl) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and OpenFlamingo (OF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Detailed information about the baselines is available in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Baselines from Hateful Memes Challenge ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The task of mitigating hateful memes is relatively new, and as such, there is a lack of substantial prior work. Thus, we evaluate our framework against a basic approach that we refer to as “Nav̈e Generation”, which focuses only on generation without assessing the type or source of hate. More specifically, this method randomly selects either the image or text component to create a substitute.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hateful memes detection task.</span> In our experiments centered on the detection task, we employ OpenCLIP (OC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, LLaVA (LV) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, Google Gemini Pro Vision (GemPro-V) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, and OpenAI GPT-4 omni (GPT-4o) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. The description, implementation details, and running time of VLMs are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Models in the Evaluation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> and <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Reproducibility ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hateful memes mitigation task.</span> GPT-4o is employed in experiments focused on the mitigation task using the seen test set consisting of 490 identified hateful memes. This model is chosen for its superior performance in hateful meme detection; however, our mitigation framework is modular and can be readily adapted to incorporate other VLMs. Using this setup, a new dataset of mitigated memes is built using our framework. Human evaluation is adopted to evaluate the mitigated memes. We choose <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of potential images considered for substitution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Hateful Memes Detection Performance</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report accuracy and AUROC on both the seen and unseen test data, comparing our method with 22 baselines, which are divided into different types. Experiments using our proposed prompts are conducted under two conditions: without OCR text and with OCR text (denoted as +OCR). The results are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Hateful Memes Detection Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Baseline results are sourced from their respective reference papers.
In the table, the highest values within each type of method are underlined, while the values showing the best performance in each column are highlighted in bold.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Detection performance using different method groups: <span id="S5.T1.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;">red</span> – baselines from the challenge host <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>; <span id="S5.T1.10.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFBF;">yellow</span> – challenge winners <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>; <span id="S5.T1.10.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EFBFCF;">purple</span> – VLM baselines with a simple prompt from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>; <span id="S5.T1.10.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFBF;">green</span> – our results. Dashes (“–") denote unavailable results. The best results within each group (or each column) are <span id="S5.T1.10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span> (or <span id="S5.T1.10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>).</span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.11" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.11.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.11.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Type</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.11.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.11.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Model</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.11.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">Test Seen</td>
<td id="S5.T1.11.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">Test Unseen</td></tr>
