</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall detection performance.</span> In the zero-shot scenario, GPT-4o, utilizing our definition-guided prompt, surpasses all baselines in terms of both accuracy and AUROC when evaluated on the seen test dataset, as demonstrated in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Hateful Memes Detection Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
On the unseen test dataset, GPT-4o also achieves the best accuracy over all baselines. In terms of AUROC, although VLMs with definition-guided prompts are less effective than other challenge winners on the unseen dataset, GPT-4o can achieve approximately <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the performance from the best challenge winner (Team HateDetectron). This is impressive for a model not specifically trained on the hateful meme dataset. In comparisons among VLMs, GPT-4o exceeds the performance of all other VLMs, outperforming the second-best model (GemPro-V) by 9.40% (6.15%) in accuracy on seen (unseen) test data, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the few-shot scenario, we observe a general trend of improved performance as the number of demonstration examples increases, a common finding in in-context learning. Similar to the zero-shot case, VLMs with our few-shot prompt continue to achieve significantly better performance compared to the baselines. Notably, our definition-guided zero-shot prompt consistently surpasses Fl, OF, and OC, even outperforming those utilizing 32-shot prompting. This is understandable given that models like Fl and OF use relatively simple instructions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, while OC performs classification solely through image-text matching, lacking the ability to process contextual information. These aspects highlight the effectiveness of our definition-guided prompt in the few-shot scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, incorporating OCR text generally improves performance for most models, such as OC, LV, or GemPro-V, with gains ranging from approximately 0.70% to 2.35% in accuracy on test sets. However, this improvement does not extend to GPT-4o, which demonstrates superior performance without the inclusion of OCR text, indicating a need for further in-depth investigation. Overall, we find that state-of-the-art models like GemPro-V and GPT-4o exhibit a robust capability to extract textual content or objects within memes, regardless of the presence of OCR text.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.F5.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="157.71" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 157.71" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,157.71) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 151.81 C 0 155.07 2.64 157.71 5.91 157.71 L 471.47 157.71 C 474.73 157.71 477.38 155.07 477.38 151.81 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 151.81 C 1.97 153.98 3.73 155.75 5.91 155.75 L 471.47 155.75 C 473.65 155.75 475.41 153.98 475.41 151.81 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 5.91 75.4)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.65em;--ltx-fo-height:5.52em;--ltx-fo-depth:5.02em;font-size:10pt;" height="145.91" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.41)" width="465.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:33.65em;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/43728.png" id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:63/63;" width="63" height="63" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></span><span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:275.6pt;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The image shows a dog with an exaggerated expression, labeled with the text "Bruce Jenner’s cat." While the image alone shows no hateful content, the text may implicitly refer to Caitlyn Jenner’s transition, using Bruce as deadnaming. Although it might be an attempt at humor, it indirectly mocks gender identity. The meme can be considered as conveying a potentially hateful message.
<br class="ltx_break">Classification: <span id="S5.F5.pic1.p1.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFBF;">hateful</span>
<br class="ltx_break">Probability of the meme being hateful (from 0 to 1): 0.7</span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.F5.pic2" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="157.71" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 157.71" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,157.71) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 151.81 C 0 155.07 2.64 157.71 5.91 157.71 L 471.47 157.71 C 474.73 157.71 477.38 155.07 477.38 151.81 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 151.81 C 1.97 153.98 3.73 155.75 5.91 155.75 L 471.47 155.75 C 473.65 155.75 475.41 153.98 475.41 151.81 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 5.91 75.4)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.65em;--ltx-fo-height:5.52em;--ltx-fo-depth:5.02em;font-size:10pt;" height="145.91" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.41)" width="465.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F5.pic2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:33.65em;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/70691.png" id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:79/41;" width="79" height="41" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></span><span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:275.6pt;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The visual part of the meme shows slices of watermelon. The textual part of the meme reads “overdose”. This word typically means consuming too much of something, often used in the context of drugs, but here it can indicate an exaggerated amount of watermelon consumption. Considering the provided definition, both the image and text do not individually or together convey a hateful message.
<br class="ltx_break">Classification: <span id="S5.F5.pic2.p1.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFBF;">non-hateful</span>
<br class="ltx_break">Probability of the meme being hateful (from 0 to 1): 0.01</span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.F5.pic3" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="157.71" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 157.71" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,157.71) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 151.81 C 0 155.07 2.64 157.71 5.91 157.71 L 471.47 157.71 C 474.73 157.71 477.38 155.07 477.38 151.81 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 151.81 C 1.97 153.98 3.73 155.75 5.91 155.75 L 471.47 155.75 C 473.65 155.75 475.41 153.98 475.41 151.81 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 5.91 75.4)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.65em;--ltx-fo-height:5.52em;--ltx-fo-depth:5.02em;font-size:10pt;" height="145.91" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.41)" width="465.62"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F5.pic3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:33.65em;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/63547.png" id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:79/53;" width="79" height="53" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></span><span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:275.6pt;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The visual component shows a person making a positive gesture in a public setting. The text says “i’m down.. with that”, which typically expresses agreement or enthusiasm. There is no apparent connection between the image and text that suggests a hateful message. The meme does not seem to target or attack any individual or group based on personal characteristics.
<br class="ltx_break">Classification: <span id="S5.F5.pic3.p1.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;">non-hateful</span>
<br class="ltx_break">Probability of the meme being hateful (from 0 to 1): 0.0</span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Examples of detecting hateful memes using GPT-4o with our definition-guided prompt (without OCR). Successful detections are highlighted in <span id="S5.F5.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFFFBF;">green</span>, whereas failed instances are indicated in <span id="S5.F5.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;">red</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Content extraction.</span> Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 Hateful Memes Detection Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents two successful and one unsuccessful example from the detection task using GPT-4o with our definition-guided prompt. It is noteworthy that these detections were performed without any OCR text input. With our prompt, GPT-4o effectively identifies objects within the memes, accurately describing them with phrases such as “a dog”, “watermelon slices”, or “a person”. Moreover, the model offers insights into expressions and actions such as “a dog with exaggerated expression” or “a person smiling”. These examples also showcase the model’s impressive capability for text extraction with very high precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning ability.</span>
As aforementioned, explainability is an advantage of VLM compared to deep learning classifiers. While traditional classifiers only give users predicted classes, VLM can additionally justify its prediction. Despite successful or failed attempts in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 Hateful Memes Detection Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, GPT-4o justifies its predictions by giving explanations as instructed in our prompt. In the first example, the model surprisingly shows its knowledge about the named person in the text, which helps determine the implicit hatefulness inside this meme. It successfully recognizes the association between the transgender person and the metaphor of calling a dog a cat, revealing an underlying mockery of the targeted individual. The second example further supports our observations. Although “overdose” usually infers the excessive use of drugs, the model does not classify it as hateful content and recognizes that the meme merely discusses food consumption. Nevertheless, there are instances where the model exhibits limitations, particularly in misinterpreting mocking language or failing to detect subtle wordplay. In the third example, GPT-4o effectively analyzes both visual and textual elements of the meme but predicts the incorrect class. This error underscores the need for enhanced analysis of wordplay in memes to improve detection accuracy in future research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Hateful Memes Mitigation Performance</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human evaluation.</span> To ensure a rigorous evaluation process, we assemble a team of seven domain experts from both the fields of AI and social sciences to design the evaluation criteria and independently assess the quality of the mitigated memes. We ensure that each meme receives at least three evaluations, with the final decision determined by the majority vote. An additional expert is invited in cases where a tiebreaker is necessary. To promote diversity among the experts, we invite individuals from various backgrounds – including different ages, races, and genders. All experts receive the same evaluation guidelines. The human evaluation focuses on assessing two key criteria: <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-hatefulness</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">image-text coherence</span>. To achieve this, we asked human experts to answer the following questions:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Q1: <span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In your opinion, is the provided meme hateful?</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Answer options: non-hateful (NH) or hateful (H).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Q2: <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In your opinion, do the image and text in the meme make sense together?</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Answer options: non-coherence (NC) or coherence (C).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The first question (Q1) corresponds to the non-hatefulness criterion, while the second (Q2) corresponds to image-text coherence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mitigation performance with <span id="S5.T2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> and Naïve Generation. Q1: non-hatefulness, Q2: image-text coherence.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
