<td id="S5.T2.6.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{401_{81.8\%}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟒𝟎𝟏</mn><mrow><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">81.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{401_{81.8\%}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{533_{84.5\%}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟓𝟑𝟑</mn><mrow><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">84.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{533_{84.5\%}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.6.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.6.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" colspan="2"># Memes</th>
<td id="S5.T2.6.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">490</td>
<td id="S5.T2.6.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">631</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We present the evaluation results of mitigated memes using <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> framework and Naïve Generation technique in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3 Hateful Memes Mitigation Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Within the <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> framework, 349 memes are identified as containing unimodal hate, while 141 memes are identified as containing multimodal hate out of 490 hateful memes used in this experiment. For multimodal hate memes, mitigation can be achieved by generating either a substitute image or text. In this study, we choose to apply both substitution strategies, resulting in a total of 631 mitigated memes. Conversely, the Naïve Generation approach involves randomly selecting either the image or the text for substitution, leading to a total of 490 mitigated memes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-hatefulness.</span> The non-hatefulness criterion (Q1) prefers the answer “non-hateful”, which indicates that the mitigated memes are not hateful. This demonstrates the efficacy of a mitigation method in transforming hateful memes into non-hateful versions. In Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3 Hateful Memes Mitigation Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, when comparing the evaluation of our proposed framework to that of Naïve Generation, we observe a significant performance improvement. For memes identified as unimodal hate, randomly choosing either the image or the text to mitigate hate is ineffective if we mistakenly target the wrong modality. For instance, if the text is the source of hate, efforts to generate substitute images will not remove the hateful content from the meme. These findings highlight the advantage of our framework compared to the naïve approach, as our methodology incorporates hate analysis tasks within the framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image-text coherence.</span> For this criterion (Q2), the preferred answer is “coherence”, indicating that the visual and textual modalities in the mitigated memes are coherent. Despite the hateful content in the provided memes, we expect human experts to assess whether a meme effectively conveys a meaningful message by evaluating the synergy between the image and text. As illustrated in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3 Hateful Memes Mitigation Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we observe that both approaches successfully generate substitute components that align well with the alternative modality, achieving positive evaluations in over 80% of cases. This demonstrates the exceptional performance of the generation task within our framework. It is important to note that Naïve Generation employs the same procedures and prompts used in <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> for generating substitutions. Therefore, it is unsurprising that both approaches exhibit similar performance outcomes, showing that our prompts for generation are robust and suitable for application deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shareable mitigated memes on social media.</span> A meme deemed appropriate for sharing on social media should be both non-hateful and exhibit image-text coherence, i.e., Q1 = “non-hateful” and Q2 = “coherence”. Utilizing our <span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span>, there are 434 mitigated memes that satisfy both criteria, comprising 68.8% of the total memes. For Naïve Generation, there are only 205 out of 490 (41.84%) mitigated memes satisfying the condition. These findings further confirm the effectiveness of hate analysis tasks in our proposed framework.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.T3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Examples of mitigated memes using <span id="S5.T3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> framework. Evaluations in <span id="S5.T3.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;">red</span> highlight cases where the framework has failed to meet the corresponding criteria. 
<br class="ltx_break">Orig. – Original; Miti. – Mitigated; Eval. – Evaluation; NH – non-hateful; H – hateful; NC – non-coherence; C – coherence.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T3.7" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:90pt;vertical-align:-43.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-98.8pt,25.8pt) scale(0.635836430457331,0.635836430457331) ;">
