<tr id="S5.T3.7.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.7.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Miti.</th>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/56790_tx_ou.png" id="S5.T3.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:96/61;" width="96" height="61" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/98637_im_ou.png" id="S5.T3.g7" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:96/72;" width="96" height="72" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/51263_tx_im_ou.png" id="S5.T3.g8" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:93/62;" width="93" height="62" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/65948_tx_ou.png" id="S5.T3.g9" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:93/62;" width="93" height="62" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><img src="2505.00150v1/figures/65948_im_ou.png" id="S5.T3.g10" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:96/64;" width="96" height="64" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.7.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.7.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Hate</th>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Unimodal hate – Text</td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Unimodal hate – Image</td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Unimodal hate – Both</td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="2">Multimodal hate</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.7.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.7.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Eval.</th>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Q1: NH, Q2: C</td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Q1: NH, <span id="S5.T3.7.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;">Q2: NC</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Q1: NH, Q2: C</td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Q1: NH, Q2: C</td>
<td id="S5.T3.7.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">Q1: NH, Q2: C</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality of mitigated memes.</span> Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Hateful Memes Mitigation Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents five examples of mitigated memes generated using the <span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> framework. From left to right, each example represents one case in our designed framework where the original meme is: (1) unimodal hate from text, (2) unimodal hate from image, (3) unimodal hate from both image and text, (4) multimodal hate – text to be mitigated, and (5) multimodal hate - image to be mitigated. In the first example, the origin of hate is from the text while the image is not hateful as it only shows a city view. Hence, our <span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> first identifies the original meme as unimodal hate (image) successfully. Then, the generated text slightly changes the context to security enhancement in Paris and delivers a positive message that “safety measures are important worldwide”. This alteration effectively removes the hateful element while ensuring the image-text coherence, as intended. Similar observations are shown in other examples, such as “recycling” in the fourth example or “pride parade” in the fifth example.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The tone of the text is preserved following mitigation in examples necessitating text substitution. For instance, the texts in example (1) both conclude with a question, those in example (2) consistently mention “friends asked for directions”, and the texts in example (3) both start with “me watching”. The task of generating substitute images also yields impressive results. In example (3), the mitigation framework combines a new image showcasing a group of friends with joyful expressions with a playful text that has been cleansed of harmful content. This observation is further supported by example (5), where the framework selects a new image of happy individuals participating in an actual pride parade.</p>
</div>
