<div id="S5.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effectiveness of hate analysis tasks.</span> In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we highlighted the critical roles of the initial two hate analysis tasks, specifically analyzing the type of hate and identifying the source of hate, within our framework. In this section, we present detailed results from <span id="S5.SS3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span> related to these two tasks, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3 Hateful Memes Mitigation Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. After analyzing the type of hate, there are 349 (141) memes classified as unimodal (multimodal) hate. Notably, each multimodal hate meme is counted twice, as it is possible to create two new mitigated memes: one through image substitution and another through text substitution, as demonstrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Hateful Memes Mitigation ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Memes with unimodal hate are further categorized based on the source of hate: 280 memes originate hate from images, 38 from text, and 31 from both. The results in columns 3-6 represent various scenarios potentially leading to hate in memes. In most cases, human evaluations indicate that the framework successfully meets a success rate exceeding 80% across all criteria. This underscores the effectiveness of our framework, highlighting the critical roles of hate analysis tasks within the entire framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our research is among the pioneering efforts to tackle the issue of detecting and mitigating hateful content in memes with VLMs. Utilizing our definition-guided prompts, pretrained VLMs demonstrate exceptional reasoning capability and content extraction for the detection task through zero-shot or few-shot prompting, eliminating the need for additional training or fine-tuning steps. Subsequently, we define a novel task focused on mitigating hateful content in memes and propose a unified framework, called <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">UnHateMeme</span>, to address this challenge. Through our framework, GPT-4o demonstrates remarkable proficiency in transforming hateful memes into non-hateful ones while maintaining image-text coherence, as validated by human evaluation. In future work, we plan to enhance the framework by integrating image generation models such as Stable Diffusion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
