<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Ethics Statement.</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large and foundation models have garnered attention from both the research community and society in recent years. In the era of AI, multimodal generative models offer significant advances in digital content creation, albeit raising concerns about unknown safety and privacy threats. The aim of this work is to evaluate how VLMs, including state-of-the-art models like LLaVA, Google Gemini, and OpenAI GPT-4o, can be utilized to explore hatefulness in multimodal memes. As memes can convey different meanings depending on the visual and textual context, we seek to detect and correct hate speech in a constructive manner. Our formulation for the mitigation task aims to pave the way for a new potential direction in which VLMs can address human mistakes or malicious intentions. Proposed prompts consist of carefully curated sentences designed to guide models toward exercising fairness, equity, and non-violent actions. The experiments were conducted using well-known datasets from prior research, and human experts involved in the evaluation were informed in advance about the offensive content in the data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Prompts</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide full details of prompts used in experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition-guided Few-shot Prompt.</span> In Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix B Prompts ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we extend our definition-guided prompt to a few-shot prompt used for experiments in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Hateful Memes Detection Performance ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Detecting and Mitigating Hateful Content in Multimodal Memes with Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.F6.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="588.46" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 588.46" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,588.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#008080;" fill="#008080" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 582.55 C 0 585.81 2.64 588.46 5.91 588.46 L 471.47 588.46 C 474.73 588.46 477.38 585.81 477.38 582.55 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#D9FFD9;" fill="#D9FFD9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 582.55 C 1.97 584.73 3.73 586.49 5.91 586.49 L 471.47 586.49 C 473.65 586.49 475.41 584.73 475.41 582.55 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 17.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:40.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="560.9" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 557.45)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.F6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User:<span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break">Hatefulness definition: Hate speech is defined as a direct or indirect attack on people based on characteristics, including ethnicity, race, nationality, immigration status, religion, caste, sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, disease, mocking hate crime or evoking pain from historical figures. The definition of attack includes violent or dehumanizing speech, statements of inferiority, and calls for exclusion or segregation.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">Classification criteria: A meme is considered hateful if either the image or text is hateful on its own. The meme is also considered hateful if both the image and text are not hateful individually, but together convey a hateful message. Conversely, if neither the image nor the text is hateful and they do not together create a hateful meaning, the meme is not considered hateful.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">Here are some demonstrations on classifying memes:

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-demonstration-meme-1&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Classification: <span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;label-1&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-demonstration-meme-2&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Classification: <span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;label-2&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-demonstration-meme-3&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Classification: <span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;label-3&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-demonstration-meme-4&gt;
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Classification: <span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;label-4&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A2.F6.pic1.1.1.1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;upload-test-meme&gt;</span>

<br class="ltx_break">For this test image, you consider visual and textual meanings to classify the meme as hateful or non-hateful based on the hatefulness definition and classification criteria.
After giving a short explanation, provide the answer with either hateful or non-hateful in the following format:
<br class="ltx_break">Classification:
<br class="ltx_break">Probability of the meme being hateful (from 0 to 1):</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A2.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Definition-guided few-shot prompt for detecting hateful memes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Baselines from Hateful Memes Challenge</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Facebook introduced the Hateful Memes Challenge in late 2020 to provide a reference benchmark dataset and common visio-linguistic baselines. Indeed, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> tested and provided 11 vision and language models through their software MMF <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          https://github.com/facebookresearch/mmf</span></span></span>. MMF (MultiModal Framework) is a framework for vision and language multimodal research with state-of-the-art models.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Unimodal</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image-Grid.</span>
The Image-Grid baseline uses features from ResNet-152’s res-5c layer after average pooling (layer of 2048 neurons). ResNet-152 is a Deep Residual Neural Network with 152 layers originally designed for image/scene classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image-Region.</span>
The Image-Region baseline uses features from the Faster-RCNN’s fc6 layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> with a ResNeXt-152 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> backbone (layer of 4096 neurons). The Fast Region-based Convolutional Neural Network is originally trained on a Visual Genome, and the resulting fc6 features are fine-tuned using weights of the fc7 layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-BERT.</span>
The Text BERT baseline is the original BERT model that outputs a vector of dimension 768. BERT is a bidirectional transformer-based model for language representation and understanding that learns embeddings for subwords <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Multimodal: unimodal pretraining</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal models from unimodal pretraining typically combine the output or the features of vision and linguistic models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Late Fusion.</span>
In the Late Fusion baseline, the output is the mean of the ResNet-152 and BERT output scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concat BERT.</span>
The Concat BERT baseline uses the concatenation of the ResNet-152 features with the BERT features, where an MLP is trained on top of it.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMBT-Grid.</span>
The MMBT-Grid baseline is the original supervised multimodal bi-transformers (MMBT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> that uses the features of Image-Grid. MMBT is a multimodal bi-transformer model that combines BERT as the textual encoder and ResNet-152 as the image encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMBT-Region.</span>
The MMBT-Grid baseline is the original supervised multimodal bi-transformers (MMBT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> that uses the features of Image-Region.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ViLBERT.</span>
Unimodally pretrained of ViLBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual BERT.</span>
Unimodally pretrained of Visual BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Multimodal: multimodal pretraining</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ViLBERT CC.</span> The ViLBERT CC baseline is the multimodally pretrained version (also the original version) of ViLBERT that was trained on Conceptual Caption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> for the tasks of sentence-image alignment, masked language modeling, and masked visual-feature classification. ViLBERT is a BERT-based model designed to learn joint contextualized representations of vision and language by using two separate transformers: one for vision and one for language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual BERT COCO.</span>
The Visual BERT COCO baseline is the multimodally pretrained version (also the original version) of Visual BERT that was trained on COCO Captions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> for the tasks of sentence-image alignment and masked language modeling. [ref to COCO]. Like ViLBERT, it is based on BERT. It was originally designed to learn joint representations of language and visual content from paired data. Unlike ViLBERT, it uses a single cross-modal transformer to align elements of the input text and regions in the input image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Challenge Winner</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the Hateful Memes Challenge, many state-of-the-art models were proposed to deal with the task. n Zhu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, who achieved the highest AUROC performance, focused on extracting and applying demographic information for model enhancement. Meanwhile, Niklas Muennighof <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> employed the Stochastic Weight Averaging method to improve model stability. Team Hatedetectron <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> applied an augmented dataset on original data to train the VisualBERT model, which achieved the best accuracy. Team Kingsterdam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> used a weighted linear combination of ensemble learners to get the final prediction instead of the majority voting technique. The multimodal deep ensemble technique is leveraged by Vlad Sandulescu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> to boost the performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Models in the Evaluation</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenCLIP (OC).</span> OpenCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> is the attempt to reproduce and train more open-source versions of CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. CLIP (Contrastive Language-Image Pre-Training) is a neural network that connects images and text. In general, the model aims to learn the relationship between the images and their textual description, such as captions or labels. We use the version ViT-G/14, named OpenCLIP-ViT-G/14 (OC-G/14).</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLaVA-Llama-2-13B (LV).</span> Pretrained CLIP and Llama-2 serve as the visual encoder and language model in this VLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, respectively. While Flamingo uses the cross-attention scheme, LLaVA proposes a projection layer to map CLIP features to language tokens. Then, both visual and textual tokens are used as inputs to the language model. The LLaVA version utilized in this work is fine-tuned on the Llama-2-13B chat model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, referred to as LLaVA-Llama-2-13B (LV) in this study.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Google Gemini Pro Vision (GemPro-V).</span> Built upon the Transformer architecture, Gemini versions are trained using advanced techniques in network design and model optimization to improve scalability. Although the model architecture is not detailed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, the visual encoder draws inspiration from Flamingo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Alongside textual data, the models are trained with inputs comprising images, audio, and videos interspersed with text. Unlike previous methods that allowed users to download and run inference on local machines, Google Gemini only allows inference via API. We collect results using Gemini Pro-Vision (GemPro-V).
Since the dataset might contain hate speech, we configured the safety setting of Gemini to the lowest level, ensuring that the API does not stop generating responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenAI GPT-4 omni (GPT-4o).</span> GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> is OpenAI’s flagship language generation model, capable of processing and generating multimodal inputs and outputs, including text, audio, images, and video. It is trained on a unified model network using multimodal training data, addressing the latency and information loss issues associated with previous versions that relied on separate model modules. GPT-4o is accessible exclusively through an API.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flamingo/OpenFlamingo (Fl/OF).</span> Flamingo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> effectively executes vision-language tasks using pretrained visual encoders and language models. The Perceiver Resampler is trained to project extensive embedding features from the encoder (NFNet-F6) to visual tokens. Leveraging a cross-attention scheme, the model processes multiple image-text inputs, enabling in-context learning. OpenFlamingo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> is an attempt to replicate Flamingo as an open-source version.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Reproducibility</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset.</span> We focus on Facebook Hateful Memes Dataset, which is publicly available for download at <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ankitgupta2001/facebook-hateful-meme-dataset" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Kaggle</a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span> Except for Google Gemini, all VLMs used in the evaluation are public models for research purpose. LLaVA, OpenCLIP and their inference codes can be accessed at <a href="https://github.com/haotian-liu/LLaVA" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">LLaVA Github</a> and <a href="https://github.com/mlfoundations/open_clip" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">OpenCLIP</a>, respectively. For Google Gemini, all model instances are accessible via their APIs at <a href="https://ai.google.dev/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Google AI Studio</a>. As of the submission date of this paper, queries to the Gemini API are free.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Running time.</span> The inference time for each sample is approximately 7.9 seconds using the LV model with a maximum output length of 512 tokens, including image loading and tokenizing time, on a single Tesla V100 GPU with 32GB RAM. The inference times for Gemini and GPT-4o are subject to the status of their respective service providers. Nevertheless, these models typically complete processing in under 5 seconds per sample, which includes the latency associated with data transmission over the internet.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
